{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Behold now, my lords</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 19:2): 'Behold now, you are lords to me, since you have passed by me.' But the correct interpretation is that it is an expression of entreaty: 'My lords, please behold the house of your servant; turn aside now to me.' And the word 'סוּרוּ' is like 'סוּרָה שְׁבָה פֹה' (Ruth 4:1), 'סוּרָה אֲדֹנִי סוּרָה אֵלַי אַל תִּירָא' (Judges 4:18)." "And the reason for <b>and you shall rise early and go on your way</b> is to tell them that they should not tarry in the city after morning, for he knew the matter of the men of the city and their wickedness, but he thought that in the light of morning they would do it. Or perhaps he saw them as wayfarers who would not tarry in the city, and said 'and you shall rise early and go on your way,' if you wish."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Rashi's comment reads אֲדֹנַי as 'my lords' addressed to the men; Ramban preserves the plural form but repoints it as singular address to God. The Hebrew quotations are left in Hebrew script per convention."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:21.814709498Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}